      "answer": "The child has a classic presentation of acute epiglottitis and is a pediatric airway emergency. Immediate management should focus on rapid but controlled airway protection, empiric antimicrobial therapy, adjunctive measures, and infection‑control precautions.\n\n**Airway stabilization**\n1. Keep the child upright, calm, and minimally handled; avoid supine positioning or attempts at oral examination that could precipitate complete obstruction.\n2. Activate the airway team (anesthesiology, otolaryngology, critical‑care nursing) and arrange for definitive airway management in an operating‑room or PICU setting equipped with a difficult‑airway cart, video‑laryngoscope, flexible fiber‑optic bronchoscope, rigid bronchoscope, and surgical‑airway instruments (cricothyrotomy set for children >8 yr, tracheostomy set for younger).\n3. Pre‑oxygenate with 100 % O₂ via a non‑rebreather mask while maintaining spontaneous ventilation.\n4. Preferred intubation technique: inhalational induction with sevoflurane (or ketamine‑based IV induction) while preserving spontaneous effort, followed by rapid‑sequence video‑laryngoscopic or fiber‑optic intubation by the most experienced provider. Blind nasotracheal intubation is contraindicated.\n5. If intubation fails, be prepared for emergent surgical airway (needle cricothyrotomy or tracheostomy) as per pediatric algorithms.\n6. Once the airway is secured, transfer the patient to the ICU for continued monitoring.\n\n**Pharmacologic therapy**\n- **Antibiotics**: Start empiric IV ceftriaxone 50–75 mg/kg (max 2 g) once daily *or* cefotaxime 50 mg/kg every 8 h to cover Haemophilus influenzae type b, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Staphylococcus aureus. Add vancomycin if MRSA risk is high.\n- **Corticosteroids**: Give dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg IV (max 10 mg) or methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg IV; although evidence is limited, steroids are commonly used to reduce supraglottic edema and are safe in this setting.\n- **Antipyretics**: Acetaminophen 15 mg/kg PO/IV for fever and comfort.\n\n**Vaccination status & counseling**\n- Note incomplete Hib immunization; Hib remains a leading pathogen in unvaccinated children. Document the gap and arrange for catch‑up Hib vaccination once the acute illness resolves.\n- Discuss the importance of age‑appropriate immunizations with the parents.\n\n**Infection‑control precautions**\n- Implement droplet precautions (surgical mask, eye protection, gown, gloves) for all staff until the causative organism is identified and appropriate isolation can be discontinued.\n- Use a private room (ideally negative‑pressure if available) for the airway procedure.\n\n**Monitoring & disposition**\n- Admit to the pediatric ICU for continuous pulse‑oximetry, cardiac monitoring, and frequent airway reassessment.\n- Repeat lateral neck X‑ray or bedside ultrasound only if clinically indicated.\n- Communicate clearly with the family about the emergent nature of the airway, the steps being taken, and the plan for ongoing care.\n\nThis plan addresses airway protection, empiric therapy, adjunctive steroids, the relevance of the child’s vaccination history, and necessary infection‑control measures.",
